# Environment files hold connection strings, keys and per-deployment choices, so the rule below keeps
# every real `.env.<environment>` out of git — which is the rule that holds in an application CLONED from
# this one, where nothing is tracked yet and `altea-clone`'s initial commit therefore leaves them out.
#
# EASTWIND'S OWN are force-added past it on purpose: `altea-clone` copies what `git ls-files` lists, so a
# tracked `.env.<environment>` is one a new application receives with the shape of its environment already
# filled in. That makes them public files in a public repository — nothing actually secret may go in one
# here, and sharing one from a cloned application is a deliberate `git add -f` by whoever owns it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Synchronization scripts written by `terminal sync` — a throwaway artefact of one
# database's drift, reviewed then applied. A migration you KEEP goes to eastwind/migrations.
terminal/sync/

# One-off end-to-end checks written against a real database while a framework module was being ported
# ("does a @quoted toString lower to SQL", "does the cached dashboard invalidate"). Each is a scratch
# script for ONE session's question, kept on disk because re-running it beats rewriting it — but not
# source this application ships, so it stays out of git (and out of Modules.xml, which asks git what
# belongs to the project).
terminal/probes/

# Playwright's own output for the last run (traces, screenshots, .last-run.json). Written by
# `pnpm --filter eastwind test`, regenerated every run.
test-results/
playwright-report/

# The default FILE STORE root: where every FilePath, attachment and cached query lands when no other path
# is configured (see app/eastwindFileStores.server.ts). Runtime output of whatever this database happens
# to hold, regenerated by using the application — never source. Keeping the folder inside the package
# rather than outside it is a convenience for a TEMPLATE application; a deployment configures its own.
files/
